[36m(RewardLoopWorker pid=2759993)[0m **Step 1: Understand the semantics**  
[36m(RewardLoopWorker pid=2759993)[0m - A node is 0 if it is not a downstream descendant of the perturbed node.  
[36m(RewardLoopWorker pid=2759993)[0m - A node is 1 if it is a downstream descendant of the perturbed node.  
[36m(RewardLoopWorker pid=2759993)[0m - A node is 0 if it is not a descendant of the perturbed node.  
[36m(RewardLoopWorker pid=2759993)[0m 
[36m(RewardLoopWorker pid=2759993)[0m **Step 2: Analyze the candidate graph**  
[36m(RewardLoopWorker pid=2759993)[0m - The edges are:  
[36m(RewardLoopWorker pid=2759993)[0m   - B -> A  
[36m(RewardLoopWorker pid=2759993)[0m   - C -> A  
[36m(RewardLoopWorker pid=2759993)[0m   - C -> B  
[36m(RewardLoopWorker pid=2759993)[0m 
[36m(RewardLoopWorker pid=2759993)[0m This means:  
[36m(RewardLoopWorker pid=2759993)[0m - A is a descendant of B and C.  
[36m(RewardLoopWorker pid=2759993)[0m - B is a descendant of C.  
[36m(RewardLoopWorker pid=2759993)[0m - C is a descendant of itself (but this is a self-loop, which is not allowed in a causal graph).  
[36m(RewardLoopWorker pid=2759993)[0m 
[36m(RewardLoopWorker pid=2759993)[0m So, the graph has the following structure:
[36m(RewardLoopWorker pid=2759993)[0m - B is an ancestor of A.
[36m(RewardLoopWorker pid=2759993)[0m - C is an ancestor of A.
[36m(RewardLoopWorker pid=2759993)[0m - C is an ancestor of B.
[36m(RewardLoopWorker pid=2759993)[0m 
[36m(RewardLoopWorker pid=2759993)[0m Thus, the descendants of B are A (direct) and C (via B → C).  
[36m(RewardLoopWorker pid=2759993)[0m The descendants of C are A (direct) and B (direct).  
[36m(RewardLoopWorker pid=2759993)[0m The descendants of A are nothing (since A is not a node that points to any other node).
[36m(RewardLoopWorker pid=2759993)[0m 
[36m(RewardLoopWorker pid=2759993)[0m ---
